Brief step 7: the gold cannot be made the unique owner, and the fault rule cannot be met. Both allowed facades are plain fixed-field formatters. Every hop in a chain beneath them must pass the whole input (name or value) and the whole output field (buf, offset, length) down to the gold. So every field-level step whose omission a test catches can be done exactly by a one-line edit at any hop:
- truncation to the field: cut the name, e1r passes the full suite;
- NUL padding: clear the field, e2r passes the full suite;
- trailing space: write it after the call;
- leading zeros: pre-fill the field with '0'.
That breaks both the single-owner rule and "no caller can pre-filter or recover".

The per-element cores have no special-case step that a correct, trace-free gold could hold. The name copy is a total low-byte cast, so any char-class decision is a dead decision (T2c). Octal digit extraction has only the shift, which freezes the value (T2b) and breaks every archive test if omitted, and the overflow decision, which no test catches.

Also, TarArchiveEntry.writeEntryHeader sends every tar header through both facades. Any omission visible on ordinary values therefore fails several archive tests (the leading-zero fill fails 7). The only single-test omissions (e1, e2, e4) are exactly the pre-filterable or recoverable ones above.

