build.reason:
parseName has two per-element steps. I tested each candidate omission directly on parseName in a scratch copy against the full suite:
- dropping the NUL rule fails all 12 reaching tests;
- dropping the 0xFF mask fails both testRoundTripNames and testCOMPRESS114, which both decode byte 0xB1;
- returning an empty name for a full field fails testName and ChangeSetTestCase::testDeleteFromTar, because the GNU magic "ustar " fills its field and the rewritten archive is no longer detected;
- bounding the scan by the buffer length instead of the field end lets a hop that truncates to `length` repair every input, so the gold would not be the single owner.

The only omission that exactly one test catches is the field-end rule. The over-read is harmless for every other reaching test: tar headers have a NUL after each name, and an over-read magic or version value is truncated back on rewrite. testName's full field runs to the buffer end and throws. The external pins are:
- the facade javadoc's "or the buffer length is reached";
- TarUtils::formatNameBytes, which fills the field without a NUL;
- ArchiveUtils::isEqual and ArchiveUtilsTest::testCompareBA, where trailing NULs are optional;
- TarArchiveInputStream::getNextTarEntry, which removes a trailing NUL only if one is present.

The ownership tests (testCompareBA, testDeleteFromTar, testRoundTripNames, testCOMPRESS114) all pass on the variant.

Mutation screen on the original facade, full suite each time:
- dropping the `> unsigned` term fails 2 tests (testVerifyHeaderCheckSum and testCOMPRESS117);
- dropping `== unsigned` fails 1 test, but in a gold that reads as a T3 one-of-two trace;
- dropping `== signed` fails 0 tests;
- dropping the field-to-spaces step fails 5 tests;
- dropping the mask fails only testCOMPRESS209.

So the mask omission is the only usable single-test fault. To stop a later hop from absorbing it, for example with Math.abs on a returned sum or a `storedSum > 0` filter, the design puts in the gold everything that touches the stored checksum or the unsigned sum: the parse, the sum and the decision. The signed alternative is computed in hop 2 and passed down as historicValue, so the gold holds one sum only.

The gold's javadoc gives the plain contract (the sum of the block's bytes, field counted as spaces) and says nothing about unsigned bytes. The convention is pinned outside the gold:
- TarUtils.computeCheckSum (`BYTE_MASK & element`);
- TarArchiveEntry.writeEntryHeader (eight spaces, then computeCheckSum written by formatCheckSumOctalBytes);
- the facade's kept javadoc ("sum of the unsigned byte values");
- hop 2's documented signed historic checksum.

The variant fails only the trigger, and the correct configuration fails nothing.

READY on the first design, with no gate-iteration redesigns.

I rejected the other natural fault: dropping the unsigned treatment, with testNegative as trigger. Any caller, the facade included, could then write the lowest digit itself and pass value >>> 3 with a field one shorter. That repair stays consistent with every javadoc, and the gold's output would still be used, so it would count as a competing repair. I chose the size-check omission because every repair above the gold has to re-derive the digit count and throw before the chain runs. On the failing call the gold's output is then never used, which is a bypass by the Math-55 precedent.

The pins for the convention outside the gold:
- the facade's javadoc "@throws IllegalArgumentException if the value will not fit in the buffer", and the same clause on formatOctalBytes, formatLongOctalBytes and formatCheckSumOctalBytes;
- TarUtils.formatLongBinary, which throws IllegalArgumentException when a value is too large for its field;
- TarArchiveOutputStream.failForBigNumber, pinned by testMaxFileSizeError and testOldEntryError;
- exact fits are accepted, pinned by testRoundTripOctal and testNegative.

The single-owner case: only the gold holds the digits that are left once the field is full. When a number fills the field exactly, the gold returns the same position (the field's start), so no caller can tell from the result that digits were dropped.

The facade is a fixed six-byte prefix equality. Which tests reach it limits the fault to a false positive at the last byte, and every one-line omission that produces one leaves a local trace. The cases:

(1) False positive at the last byte. This is the only failure seen by testSignatureCheck and by no other test. On the trigger the two arrays differ only at index 5. The variant must therefore either:
- not compare that byte (coverage);
- compare it lossily (0x1C and 0x1D differ only in bit 0, and no natural normalisation equates them);
- compare it with the wrong position, which breaks the true cases; or
- exit early, which needs a data-dependent exit and none exists for a fixed signature.
Only coverage works. If the omitted line is the only comparison, the gold no longer reads buffer or signature (T1). Otherwise the remaining comparisons visibly treat indices 0..k and leave the tail of the signature's closed index set untreated: T3(a), and T3(c) when the javadoc names the signature's bytes. Formulations checked:
- word loop plus tail loop (version 1; the independent checker flagged T3);
- last-byte-first check followed by a pre-decrement reverse loop (the variant starts at length-1);
- overlapping final word with a clamp (dropping the clamp throws AIOOBE in testSignatureCheck and in detectsAndThrowsFor7z, so G5 fails);
- head bytes then words (the omission loses bytes 0-1, which no test detects);
- int/long accumulators, reset or flush steps, XOR/OR folds (each a frozen local, a dead local, or no effect);
- a template copy prefilled with the signature (the same partial-word trace).

(2) False negative. testSignatureCheck and detectsAndThrowsFor7z both pass the exact 7z signature on the true path, so a false negative fails both (G5). The only exceptions:
- Exact fit (6-byte arrays against the factory's 12-byte buffer). A gold that compares the whole rest of the buffer fails only detectsAndThrowsFor7z, but any hop can trim the buffer to the signature's length, a one-line edit consistent with every hop's docs (a competing repair).
- Aliasing (assertions 1-2 pass sevenZSignature itself). Read-only code is unaffected.
- length greater than the array length (assertion 2 only). The gold would have to use length, and a hop clamp Math.min(length, data.length) is a competing repair.

(3) Moving the length guard into the gold and omitting it (assertion 3). Any hop can re-add `if (length < sig.length) return false;`, which is a competing repair. A variant comparing min(length, signature.length) bytes also reads as partial (T3 doubt).

So every fault that keeps a single owner (an omission in the byte comparison) carries T3, or T1 if nothing is left to compare. Every trace-free alternative is either repairable at a hop or fails a second test. Brief step 7 / batch-3 rule: no valid fault without a local trace exists for this target.

