This is brief step 7, "no fault that only one test catches". NodePointer.newNodePointer is a short dispatcher (pool n_lines 22). It has three decisions: the null-bean shortcut, the first-non-null acceptance, and the no-factory throw. The loop over the factories is the only iteration, and it has no element-level special case. Screen results on the full suite (baseline 0 failing):
- removing the null shortcut fails 51 tests;
- removing the acceptance test fails 270;
- the throw path is reached by no test (0 failing when it is replaced).
So no omission yields a single newly failing existing test (gate G5). The null shortcut and the throw are also steps a hop above the gold can redo (a null check before the gold, a throw on a null result), so they also break the single-owner rule. Building a richer computation into the chain would mean reimplementing factory logic from other classes, which is not a redistribution of the facade's computation. No other allowed target exists for this instance.

